.TH CLEVO-CC 1 "2026-09-13" "clevo-cc-linux 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clevo-cc \- command-line client for the Clevo fan and performance controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clevo-cc
[\fB\-\-transport\fR \fIkall|acpi-call|dbus\fR]
[\fB\-\-fixture\fR \fIFIle\fR]
\fISubcommand\fR [\fIOptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clevo-cc
reads fan status, the fan curve and the current modes. It is dry-run by default:
every write needs
.B \-\-apply
and, when talking to
.BR clevod ,
a PolicyKit authorization.
.SH TRANSPORTS
.TP
.B mock
(default with
.BR \-\-fixture )
Replay a fixture; never touches hardware.
.TP
.B acpi-call
Read-only, via
.IR /proc/acpi/call .
Rejects every write before any I/O.
.TP
.B dbus
Talk to
.BR clevod
over
.BR org.clevo.CC ;
reads need no privilege, writes go through PolicyKit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clevo\-cc \-\-fixture fixtures/example.fixture fan status
sudo clevo\-cc \-\-transport acpi\-call fan watch \-\-interval\-ms 1000
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan status
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan set\-mode max \-\-apply
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan set\-curve \-\-cpu "40,20 60,40 80,70 100,100" \-\-apply
.fi
.SH FAN CURVES
.B fan set\-curve
writes a four\-point curve (command 14) and selects the
.B custom
fan mode, which is what makes the firmware use it.
Points are given as
.IR "temp,duty" ,
with duty in percent; temperatures must strictly increase.
.B \-\-gpu1
defaults to the CPU curve and
.B \-\-gpu2
to an all\-zero curve, which leaves that channel alone.
.PP
Command 14 replaces the whole table, so the stack reads the current curve first
and merges only the channels you name; channels you omit keep their values.
.PP
Selecting the custom curve is a separate step in the driver interface, but
.B set\-curve
does it for you: writing a curve without selecting
.B custom
would leave the firmware on its auto curve.
.PP
Only the middle two points of each fan reach the EC; it keeps its own first and
last point. All four are still required so the curve you send is a complete
description, and the two that are not written are accepted as given.
.SH SAFETY
Writes need
.B \-\-apply
and, over D\-Bus, a PolicyKit authorization; without an authentication agent
the daemon denies non\-root callers rather than silently allowing them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clevod (8)
